referenceSolutions.python:
def catalogBranchesPositiveProofStrip(root, k):
    a=root
    out=[]
    def visit(node):
        if not node: return
        if node.val>k: visit(node.left)
        if k<=node.val<=k+12: out.append(node.val)
        if node.val<k+12: visit(node.right)
    visit(a)
    return [x for x in out if x>0]

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int[] catalogBranchesPositiveProofStrip(TreeNode root, int k) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Walk {void visit(TreeNode node){if(node==null)return;if(node.val>k)visit(node.left);if(node.val>=k&&node.val<=k+12)out.add(node.val);if(node.val<k+12)visit(node.right);}}new Walk().visit(a);}
    return out.stream().filter(x->x>0).mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

root is a binary search tree with no repeated values. In this tree, all values in a left branch are smaller than its parent, and all values in a right branch are larger. Find values from k through k + 12, including both ends. Put them in the result list from smallest to largest. Skip branches whose values cannot be in this range. An empty tree gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Return only numbers greater than 0 from the result list. Keep their order and any repeated numbers. If none matches, return [].

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(h + r); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes; h is the longest root-to-leaf path in the tree; a leaf is a node with no children; r is the number of tree values found. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

